window.EN = window.EN || {};
window.EN.lexikon = (window.EN.lexikon || []).concat([
  { key:"Psychotherapie", begriff: "Psychotherapy", kurz: "Treatment of mental disorders and of suffering with psychological components using scientifically grounded psychological means within a professional relationship.", text: "Psychotherapy refers to the targeted, methodologically grounded treatment of mental disorders of clinical significance and of the psychological components of physical illnesses. Its means are primarily communicative and relationship-based: conversation, practice, experience, reflection and targeted behavior change, not pharmacological or surgical interventions.\n\nIn Germany, the practice of psychotherapy as a healing profession is regulated by law; the Psychotherapists Act (Psychotherapeutengesetz) defines it as any activity carried out by means of scientifically recognized psychotherapeutic procedures for the assessment, cure or alleviation of disorders of clinical significance. Psychological counseling, coaching or the handling of social conflicts without clinical significance do not fall under this definition.\n\nPsychotherapy research has demonstrated the efficacy of psychotherapy for a broad spectrum of mental disorders. A matter of ongoing debate is the extent to which specific techniques, as opposed to common factors such as the therapeutic relationship, contribute to treatment success." },
  { key:"Richtlinienverfahren", begriff: "Richtlinienverfahren (Guideline Procedures)", kurz: "The psychotherapy procedures approved in the Psychotherapy Guideline of the Federal Joint Committee and therefore reimbursed by German statutory health insurance.", text: "In Germany, Richtlinienverfahren (guideline procedures, i.e., procedures reimbursed by statutory health insurance) are the psychotherapy procedures that the Federal Joint Committee (Gemeinsamer Bundesausschuss, G-BA) has approved in the Psychotherapy Guideline for the care of persons with statutory health insurance. Besides scientific recognition (usually by the Scientific Advisory Board on Psychotherapy), the prerequisite is proof of benefit, medical necessity and cost-effectiveness.\n\nFour procedures are currently approved: behavior therapy, psychodynamic psychotherapy (tiefenpsychologisch fundierte Psychotherapie) and psychoanalytic psychotherapy (the latter two grouped together as psychoanalytically based procedures), and, since 2020, systemic therapy for adults; the G-BA subsequently extended the recognition of systemic therapy to children and adolescents as well.\n\nProcedures such as client-centered psychotherapy, Gestalt therapy or psychodrama are not guideline procedures. Individual methods, for example EMDR for posttraumatic stress disorder, may be applied within the framework of a guideline procedure." },
  { key:"Psychotherapie-Richtlinie", begriff: "Psychotherapy Guideline (Psychotherapie-Richtlinie)", kurz: "Directive issued by the Federal Joint Committee that regulates the requirements, procedures, service types and scope of outpatient psychotherapy within German statutory health insurance.", text: "The Psychotherapy Guideline (Psychotherapie-Richtlinie) of the Federal Joint Committee (G-BA) specifies which procedures may be provided as a benefit of statutory health insurance, for which indications psychotherapy is warranted, and which service types and session quotas apply.\n\nThe structural reform of 2017 introduced the psychotherapeutic consultation hour (Sprechstunde), acute treatment and relapse prevention; at the same time, short-term therapy was divided into two segments and group therapy was facilitated. In addition, the Psychotherapy Agreement (Psychotherapie-Vereinbarung) regulates qualification requirements as well as the application and expert review procedure.\n\nThe Guideline distinguishes the guideline procedures from methods that may only be applied within the framework of a procedure, and from practice-based and suggestive interventions such as autogenic training, progressive muscle relaxation and hypnosis." },
  { key:"Psychotherapeutengesetz", begriff: "Psychotherapists Act (Psychotherapeutengesetz)", kurz: "German federal law regulating access to the healing profession of psychotherapist, the state license (Approbation) and training; fundamentally reformed in 2019, in force since September 1, 2020.", text: "The first Psychotherapists Act (PsychThG) of 1998 came into force in 1999 and created the healing professions of psychological psychotherapist and child and adolescent psychotherapist. Access required a completed university degree (usually in psychology or, for child and adolescent psychotherapy, also in (social) education) followed by several years of postgraduate training, mostly self-financed.\n\nThe Act on the Reform of Psychotherapist Training of November 2019, in force since September 1, 2020, fundamentally reorganized training: an independent university program leading to licensure (a polyvalent bachelor’s and a master’s degree in psychotherapy) concludes with the state psychotherapeutic examination and leads to the Approbation (state license). The professional title is now uniformly “psychotherapist” (Psychotherapeutin/Psychotherapeut).\n\nThe specialist qualification (Fachkunde) required to treat persons with statutory health insurance is subsequently acquired in further specialist training regulated by state law, which takes place in paid employment. Transitional provisions apply until 2032 (in cases of hardship until 2035) for persons who began their training under the old law." },
  { key:"Approbation", begriff: "Approbation (State License)", kurz: "State authorization for the independent practice of an academic healing profession; in psychotherapy, a prerequisite for using the protected professional title.", text: "The Approbation is the state authorization for the permanent and self-responsible practice of a healing profession. It is granted by the competent state authority once the statutory requirements are met: training, a passed state examination, health-related fitness and personal reliability.\n\nSince the reform of the Psychotherapists Act (in force since September 1, 2020), the Approbation as a psychotherapist is granted after a bachelor’s and master’s program with a psychotherapeutic focus and the passed psychotherapeutic examination. To bill statutory health insurance, completed further training as a specialist psychotherapist (for adults, for children and adolescents, or for neuropsychological psychotherapy) and entry in the physician register (Arztregister) are additionally required.\n\nUnder the old law, the Approbation as a psychological psychotherapist or child and adolescent psychotherapist was granted after postgraduate training concluding with a state examination; these licenses remain valid." },
  { key:"Wissenschaftlicher Beirat Psychotherapie", begriff: "Scientific Advisory Board on Psychotherapy (Wissenschaftlicher Beirat Psychotherapie)", kurz: "Joint body of the German Federal Chamber of Psychotherapists and the German Medical Association that issues expert opinions on the scientific recognition of psychotherapeutic procedures and methods.", text: "The Scientific Advisory Board on Psychotherapy (Wissenschaftlicher Beirat Psychotherapie, WBP) was established on the basis of the Psychotherapists Act and is composed in equal parts of medical and psychological or psychotherapeutic experts. In cases of doubt, authorities rely on its expert opinions regarding the scientific recognition of a procedure.\n\nOn the basis of a published methods paper, the Board examines whether sufficient evidence of efficacy exists for a procedure or method in defined areas of application. A distinction is made between psychotherapy procedures with a comprehensive treatment scope and methods that are recognized only for circumscribed indications.\n\nScientific recognition by the WBP must be distinguished from approval under social law by the Federal Joint Committee: client-centered psychotherapy, for instance, was scientifically recognized but is not a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren)." },
  { key:"Verhaltenstherapie", begriff: "Behavior Therapy", kurz: "Psychotherapy procedure based on learning theory and empirical psychology that changes problematic experience and behavior through targeted, testable interventions.", text: "Behavior therapy emerged in the 1950s and 1960s from the application of learning-theory principles (classical and operant conditioning, later observational learning) to clinical problems. Important pioneers included Joseph Wolpe, Hans J. Eysenck and Arnold Lazarus. With the cognitive turn of the 1960s and 1970s, cognitions were incorporated as conditions of behavior, so that today one usually speaks of cognitive behavioral therapy.\n\nIts hallmarks are an orientation toward empirical psychology, a problem- and goal-oriented approach, functional analysis of the problem behavior, transparency toward patients, and evaluation of treatment success. Typical interventions are exposure, cognitive restructuring, behavioral activation, problem-solving training and social skills training.\n\nIn Germany, behavior therapy is a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren, reimbursed by statutory health insurance). For numerous mental disorders it is the most extensively studied procedure, with a good evidence base." },
  { key:"Kognitive Verhaltenstherapie", begriff: "Cognitive Behavioral Therapy", kurz: "Integration of behavioral and cognitive approaches that explains and treats mental disorders through the interplay of thoughts, emotions, bodily reactions and behavior.", text: "Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) combines behavior therapy grounded in learning theory with the cognitive therapy approaches of Aaron T. Beck and Albert Ellis. Its basic assumption is that it is not events themselves but their appraisal that largely determines emotional and behavioral reactions, and that dysfunctional patterns of thinking and behavior maintain mental disorders.\n\nThe approach is structured, time-limited, collaborative and present-focused. On the basis of a case conceptualization, disorder-specific interventions are used, such as psychoeducation, cognitive restructuring, behavioral experiments, exposure, behavioral activation and relapse prevention; homework assignments serve transfer into everyday life.\n\nFor depression, anxiety disorders, obsessive-compulsive disorder, posttraumatic stress disorder, eating disorders and many other conditions, there is a broad evidence base supported by randomized controlled trials and meta-analyses. Disorder-specific CBT programs are recommended as first-line treatment in numerous national and international guidelines." },
  { key:"Dritte Welle der Verhaltenstherapie", begriff: "Third Wave of Behavior Therapy", kurz: "Umbrella term for behavioral approaches developed since the 1990s that emphasize mindfulness, acceptance, values and the function rather than the content of inner experiences.", text: "The term goes back to Steven C. Hayes (2004), who divided the development of behavior therapy into three waves: a first, learning-theory-based behavioral wave, a second, cognitive wave, and a third, contextual wave. Third-wave approaches aim less at changing the content of thoughts and feelings than at changing one’s relationship to them and their function in the context of one’s life.\n\nThe most important representatives include Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, Dialectical Behavior Therapy, Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy, Metacognitive Therapy, Functional Analytic Psychotherapy, Schema Therapy, CBASP and Compassion Focused Therapy, although the classification of individual approaches is inconsistent.\n\nIt is critically debated whether this constitutes a genuine paradigm shift or a further development of cognitive behavioral therapy. The evidence base is heterogeneous across approaches and considerably better established for some (for example, DBT for borderline personality disorder) than for others." },
  { key:"Akzeptanz- und Commitment-Therapie", begriff: "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy", kurz: "Behavioral approach developed by Steven C. Hayes and colleagues that fosters psychological flexibility through acceptance, mindfulness and values-based action.", text: "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT, pronounced as the word “act”) was developed from the 1980s onward by Steven C. Hayes, Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson. It is based on functional contextualism and Relational Frame Theory, a behavioral theory of language and cognition.\n\nIts central assumption is that psychological suffering arises primarily from psychological inflexibility: from cognitive fusion with thoughts and from experiential avoidance of unpleasant inner experiences. The therapeutic model (the “hexaflex”) comprises six processes: acceptance, cognitive defusion, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. Methodologically, ACT works with metaphors, experiential exercises and mindfulness.\n\nACT is transdiagnostic in design and has been studied for depression, anxiety disorders, chronic pain and substance use, among other conditions. Meta-analyses support its efficacy relative to control conditions and an efficacy comparable to established CBT in many areas. Questions of meaning and values link ACT thematically with logotherapy." },
  { key:"Kognitive Defusion", begriff: "Cognitive Defusion", kurz: "Process in Acceptance and Commitment Therapy in which thoughts are experienced as passing mental events rather than literal truths or instructions for action.", text: "In Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, cognitive fusion refers to the state in which thoughts dominate behavior because they are equated with reality. Cognitive defusion aims to loosen this dominance without refuting the content of the thoughts.\n\nTypical techniques include prefacing thoughts with phrases such as “I am having the thought that …”, repeating a word aloud until it loses its meaning, naming recurring thought stories, or visualization exercises such as leaves on a stream.\n\nDefusion thus differs from cognitive restructuring, which examines the truth and usefulness of thoughts. Experimental studies suggest that defusion techniques can reduce the believability of and distress caused by negative thoughts in the short term." },
  { key:"Relational Frame Theory", begriff: "Relational Frame Theory", kurz: "Behavior-analytic theory of human language and cognition that describes the learning of arbitrary relations between stimuli as the basis of symbolic thought.", text: "Relational Frame Theory (RFT) was formulated by Steven C. Hayes, Dermot Barnes-Holmes and Bryan Roche as a further development of Skinner’s behavior analysis. It postulates that humans learn to relate stimuli to one another (for example through coordination, opposition, comparison, hierarchy, temporal or deictic relations), even when these relations are not based on physical properties.\n\nCentral to the theory is the transformation of stimulus functions: when one stimulus acquires a meaning, the functions of other stimuli can also change via derived relations. RFT thereby explains why language can generate suffering, for instance when memories or words alone trigger strong aversive reactions.\n\nRFT forms the theoretical foundation of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy. Its basic assumptions have been well studied experimentally; its delineation from other theories of derived stimulus relations continues to be debated in basic research." },
  { key:"Psychologische Flexibilität", begriff: "Psychological Flexibility", kurz: "The ability to stay consciously in contact with inner experiences in the present moment and to persist in or change one’s behavior in the service of personal values.", text: "Psychological flexibility is the central target construct of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy. It comprises the six processes of acceptance, cognitive defusion, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action, which together enable values-based behavior appropriate to the situation.\n\nIts opposite, psychological inflexibility, is characterized by experiential avoidance, cognitive fusion, entanglement in the past or future, attachment to a rigid self-concept, lack of clarity about values and withdrawal from action, and is regarded as a transdiagnostic vulnerability factor.\n\nThe construct is assessed with questionnaires such as the Acceptance and Action Questionnaire, among others, whose construct validity is, however, critically debated. Conceptually, it overlaps with resilience and emotion regulation." },
  { key:"Werte (ACT)", begriff: "Values (ACT)", kurz: "In Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, freely chosen, ongoing qualities of action that give life direction and meaning.", text: "In Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, values are understood as self-chosen life directions which, unlike goals, can never be definitively achieved but only continuously lived. A value such as caring, for example, can be expressed in many concrete goals and actions.\n\nValues are clarified through exercises, analyses of life domains and metaphors; committed action in line with these values is then planned. At the same time, values serve as motivation to accept unpleasant inner experiences when these arise on the way to a meaningful life.\n\nIn terms of content, there are points of contact with the concept of values in Viktor E. Frankl’s logotherapy, which ties the discovery of meaning to the realization of creative, experiential and attitudinal values; the theoretical foundations of the two approaches, however, differ markedly." },
  { key:"Schematherapie", begriff: "Schema Therapy", kurz: "Integrative psychotherapy approach developed by Jeffrey Young for treating enduring maladaptive patterns of experience and behavior that originated in childhood and adolescence.", text: "Schema Therapy was developed from the late 1980s onward by Jeffrey Young as an extension of cognitive therapy for patients with personality disorders and chronic problems. It integrates elements of cognitive behavioral therapy, attachment theory, Gestalt therapy and psychodynamic concepts.\n\nIts basic assumption is that unmet core emotional needs in childhood lead to the development of early maladaptive schemas, which in adulthood are maintained by coping styles such as surrender, avoidance or overcompensation. The later mode model describes current emotional states as schema modes.\n\nTherapeutically, cognitive, experiential (imagery rescripting, chair dialogues) and behavioral techniques are used, together with a therapeutic relationship shaped by limited reparenting. Positive randomized trials are available for borderline personality disorder; the evidence base for other personality disorders and chronic Axis I disorders is growing. In Germany, it is applied within the framework of behavior therapy." },
  { key:"Frühe maladaptive Schemata", begriff: "Early Maladaptive Schemas", kurz: "In Schema Therapy, pervasive patterns that develop early in life, consisting of memories, emotions, cognitions and bodily sensations about oneself and relationships, which become dysfunctional later in life.", text: "According to Jeffrey Young, early maladaptive schemas develop when core emotional needs (for example, for secure attachment, autonomy, expression of feelings, spontaneity and realistic limits) remain persistently unmet in childhood and adolescence. Temperament and adverse life experiences interact in this process.\n\nYoung describes 18 schemas assigned to five domains: disconnection and rejection, impaired autonomy and performance, impaired limits, other-directedness, and overvigilance and inhibition. Examples are the schemas of abandonment/instability, mistrust/abuse, emotional deprivation, defectiveness/shame and unrelenting standards.\n\nSchemas are stabilized by coping styles such as surrender, avoidance and overcompensation. Diagnostically, they are assessed with the Young Schema Questionnaire, among other instruments; the factor structure of the domains is still being examined empirically." },
  { key:"Schemamodi", begriff: "Schema Modes", kurz: "In Schema Therapy, a person’s currently active emotional, cognitive and behavioral states resulting from activated schemas and coping responses.", text: "The mode model was developed to make the rapid switching between states understandable and treatable in severely impaired patients with many simultaneously active schemas. A mode describes which schemas and coping strategies are currently shaping experience.\n\nA distinction is made between child modes (e.g., vulnerable, angry or impulsive child), dysfunctional coping modes (e.g., detached protector, compliant surrenderer, overcompensator), dysfunctional internalized parent modes (punitive, demanding), and the functional modes of the healthy adult and the happy child.\n\nThe aim is to strengthen the healthy adult mode, care for the needs of the vulnerable child and limit punitive modes. Specific mode models have been formulated and empirically tested for borderline personality disorder." },
  { key:"Dialektisch-Behaviorale Therapie", begriff: "Dialectical Behavior Therapy", kurz: "Cognitive-behavioral treatment program developed by Marsha M. Linehan, originally for chronically suicidal patients with borderline personality disorder, which dialectically combines change and acceptance.", text: "Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha M. Linehan from the late 1970s onward and published as a manual in 1993. It is based on a biosocial model that understands borderline problems as the result of emotional vulnerability interacting with an invalidating environment and places the disturbance of emotion regulation at its center.\n\nStandard DBT comprises individual therapy, group skills training with the modules of mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, telephone coaching for crises, and a consultation team of the therapists. Treatment follows a hierarchy of targets headed by suicidal and self-injurious behavior; validation and change strategies are balanced dialectically.\n\nDBT is the most thoroughly studied treatment for borderline personality disorder and is recommended in guidelines. Further developments exist, among others, for adolescents (DBT-A) and for complex posttraumatic disorders (DBT-PTSD)." },
  { key:"Mentalisierungsbasierte Therapie", begriff: "Mentalization-Based Treatment", kurz: "Psychodynamically grounded approach based on attachment theory that fosters the ability to understand one’s own and others’ behavior as an expression of mental states.", text: "Mentalization-Based Treatment (MBT) was developed by Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy, originally for patients with borderline personality disorder. It is based on the concept of mentalizing: the ability to understand oneself and others in terms of feelings, desires, intentions and beliefs.\n\nAccording to the model, mentalizing develops in the context of secure attachment; under attachment stress it collapses in vulnerable individuals, and prementalistic modes of experience appear (psychic equivalence, pretend mode, teleological mode). The therapy combines individual and group sessions; the therapeutic stance is curious, not-knowing and directed at restoring mentalizing in the here and now.\n\nRandomized trials demonstrate the efficacy of MBT for borderline personality disorder; adaptations exist for adolescents, families and eating disorders, among others." },
  { key:"Übertragungsfokussierte Psychotherapie", begriff: "Transference-Focused Psychotherapy", kurz: "Manualized psychodynamic therapy developed by Otto F. Kernberg and colleagues for personality disorders at the borderline level of organization, which works with the transference in the here and now.", text: "Transference-Focused Psychotherapy (TFP) is based on Kernberg’s object relations theory. It assumes that personality disorders at the borderline level are characterized by identity diffusion and by split, non-integrated representations of self and others.\n\nFollowing a treatment contract that regulates the framework and the handling of self-harming behavior, the dominant object relations dyads activated in the therapeutic relationship are worked through by means of clarification, confrontation and interpretation. The goal is the integration of split images of self and object and thus a more stable identity.\n\nRandomized trials with positive results are available for TFP in borderline personality disorder." },
  { key:"CBASP", begriff: "CBASP", kurz: "Cognitive Behavioral Analysis System of Psychotherapy: an approach developed by James P. McCullough specifically for chronic depression.", text: "The Cognitive Behavioral Analysis System of Psychotherapy (CBASP) was developed by James P. McCullough Jr. as a disorder-specific therapy for chronic depression. It integrates behavioral, cognitive, interpersonal and psychodynamic elements.\n\nMcCullough assumes that people with early-onset chronic depression, often as a consequence of early traumatization, remain stuck in a preoperational style of thinking and fail to perceive the consequences of their behavior for others. Central techniques are situational analysis, the significant other history with transference hypotheses derived from it, the interpersonal discrimination exercise and the therapist’s disciplined personal involvement; the Kiesler circle serves as the framework model.\n\nCBASP has been examined in several randomized trials in chronic depression and is listed in the German National Disease Management Guideline on Unipolar Depression (Nationale VersorgungsLeitlinie) as an option for chronic courses. In Germany, it is applied within the framework of behavior therapy." },
  { key:"Metakognitive Therapie", begriff: "Metacognitive Therapy", kurz: "Approach developed by Adrian Wells that changes not the content of thoughts but beliefs about thinking and the perseverative thinking style of rumination and worry.", text: "Metacognitive Therapy (MCT) is based on the Self-Regulatory Executive Function model of Adrian Wells and Gerald Matthews. Mental disorders are traced back to the so-called cognitive attentional syndrome: persistent rumination and worry, threat-focused attention and dysfunctional coping strategies such as thought suppression.\n\nThis pattern is maintained by positive metacognitions (e.g., “Worrying helps me to be prepared”) and negative metacognitions about the uncontrollability and dangerousness of thoughts. Interventions include the Attention Training Technique, detached mindfulness, rumination postponement and behavioral experiments targeting metacognitive beliefs.\n\nMCT has been studied primarily in generalized anxiety disorder, depression and posttraumatic stress disorder; the available studies are promising but stem to a considerable extent from the developers’ own research group." },
  { key:"Achtsamkeit", begriff: "Mindfulness", kurz: "Intentional, nonjudgmental attention to experience in the present moment; as an attitude and a practice, a component of numerous psychotherapeutic approaches.", text: "The concept of mindfulness (German: Achtsamkeit) originates in Buddhist meditation traditions and was introduced in secular form into medicine and psychotherapy from the late 1970s onward, largely by Jon Kabat-Zinn. Bishop and colleagues proposed an operational definition with two components: self-regulation of attention toward immediate experience, and an attitude of curiosity, openness and acceptance.\n\nMindfulness is cultivated through formal exercises (breath meditation, body scan, mindful movement) and informal practice in everyday life. It is a central component of MBSR, MBCT, DBT and ACT and is also used as an element in many other approaches.\n\nMeta-analyses show positive effects of mindfulness-based interventions on stress, anxiety and depressive symptoms, although effects are smaller relative to active control conditions. Mechanisms of action under discussion include reduced rumination, improved emotion regulation and decentering, as well as possible adverse effects of intensive practice." },
  { key:"Achtsamkeitsbasierte Kognitive Therapie", begriff: "Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy", kurz: "Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT): an eight-week group program that combines mindfulness exercises with elements of cognitive therapy for relapse prevention in recurrent depression.", text: "Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy was developed in the 1990s by Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale. Its starting point was the observation that in people with repeated depressive episodes, even mild deteriorations in mood can reactivate depressive thought patterns.\n\nThe program builds on MBSR and comprises eight weekly group sessions with daily practice. The aim is not to change the content of thoughts but to cultivate a decentered stance in which thoughts are recognized as mental events, as well as to recognize early warning signs of relapse.\n\nRandomized trials and meta-analyses demonstrate that MBCT lowers the risk of relapse, particularly in individuals with three or more previous depressive episodes; guidelines recommend MBCT for relapse prevention." },
  { key:"Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction", begriff: "Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction", kurz: "Eight-week group program for stress management through mindfulness (MBSR) developed by Jon Kabat-Zinn.", text: "Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR) was developed in 1979 by Jon Kabat-Zinn at the University of Massachusetts Medical School, initially for patients with chronic pain and stress-related illnesses.\n\nThe program comprises eight weekly group sessions, a day of practice and daily exercises at home. Its central elements are the body scan, sitting meditation, mindful body exercises drawn from hatha yoga, and the transfer of mindfulness into everyday life.\n\nMBSR is not a psychotherapy procedure in the narrow sense but a psychoeducational, practice-based health promotion program. Meta-analyses show positive effects on stress, anxiety and depressive symptoms, as well as on quality of life in physical illnesses. MBSR formed the basis for Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy." },
  { key:"Compassion Focused Therapy", begriff: "Compassion Focused Therapy", kurz: "Approach developed by Paul Gilbert, grounded in evolutionary psychology, that cultivates self-compassion in order to reduce shame and self-criticism.", text: "Compassion Focused Therapy (CFT) was developed by Paul Gilbert for people who, despite cognitive insight, suffer from pronounced shame and self-criticism and are unable to experience themselves as soothing.\n\nIt is based on a model of three affect-regulation systems: a threat and protection system, a drive and resource-seeking system, and a soothing and affiliation system. Psychological problems are understood as an imbalance in favor of the threat system. Compassionate Mind Training uses breathing and imagery exercises, the development of a compassionate self, and compassionate letters or chair work.\n\nCFT is used transdiagnostically. Studies show favorable effects on self-criticism and shame; the evidence base from large randomized trials is still limited." },
  { key:"Emotionsfokussierte Therapie", begriff: "Emotion-Focused Therapy", kurz: "Humanistic-experiential, empirically grounded approach that uses emotions as the central basis of meaning and change.", text: "Emotion-Focused Therapy (EFT) was developed by Leslie S. Greenberg together with Laura Rice and Robert Elliott out of client-centered and Gestalt therapy (initially as process-experiential therapy). It combines an empathic therapeutic relationship with targeted, marker-guided interventions.\n\nA distinction is made between primary adaptive, primary maladaptive, secondary and instrumental emotions. Change occurs through accessing and expressing emotions, regulating them, and transforming maladaptive emotions by means of adaptive emotions. Typical techniques are two-chair dialogues for self-criticism and empty-chair work for unfinished relational business.\n\nEFT has been well studied for depression; a distinct variant is Emotionally Focused Couple Therapy, founded by Sue Johnson." },
  { key:"Psychoanalyse", begriff: "Psychoanalysis", kurz: "Theory of mental life, research method and treatment approach founded by Sigmund Freud that focuses on unconscious conflicts and their effects on experience and behavior.", text: "Psychoanalysis emerged around 1900 from Sigmund Freud’s work with neurotic patients. Freud understood it simultaneously as a method for investigating unconscious mental processes, as a treatment approach and as a psychological theory. Central concepts are the dynamic unconscious, drive and defense, the structural model of id, ego and superego, transference and resistance.\n\nIn classical technique, the patient, lying on the couch, voices all associations as unfiltered as possible (free association), while the analyst listens with evenly suspended attention and interprets transference, resistance and conflicts. Numerous schools emerged from psychoanalysis, such as ego psychology, object relations theory, self psychology, and relational and intersubjective approaches.\n\nIn Germany, psychoanalytic psychotherapy and psychodynamic psychotherapy (tiefenpsychologisch fundierte Psychotherapie) constitute the psychoanalytically based guideline procedures (Richtlinienverfahren). Empirical research supports the efficacy of psychodynamic therapies for various disorders, although the body of studies on high-frequency long-term treatments is methodologically more challenging." },
  { key:"Psychodynamische Psychotherapie", begriff: "Psychodynamic Psychotherapy", kurz: "Umbrella term for psychoanalytically based forms of treatment that work on unconscious conflicts, relational patterns and structural capacities in the context of life-historical development.", text: "Psychodynamic psychotherapy encompasses the variety of approaches that emerged from psychoanalysis, ranging from high-frequency long-term analysis to focused short-term therapies. What they share is the assumption that present symptoms and relational difficulties are connected with unconscious conflicts, internalized relational experiences and limitations of psychic structure.\n\nTherapeutically, the focus is on working through recurring relational patterns, transference and countertransference, and defense, as well as fostering insight and structural capacities. More recent manualized variants include Transference-Focused Psychotherapy, Mentalization-Based Treatment, structure-oriented psychotherapy and Luborsky’s supportive-expressive therapy.\n\nMeta-analyses support the efficacy of psychodynamic therapies for depression, anxiety disorders and personality disorders; direct comparisons with cognitive-behavioral approaches often show comparable effects, although the overall number of high-quality studies is smaller." },
  { key:"Tiefenpsychologisch fundierte Psychotherapie", begriff: "Psychodynamic Psychotherapy (Tiefenpsychologisch fundierte Psychotherapie)", kurz: "Psychoanalytically based guideline procedure that concentrates on currently active unconscious conflicts and relational patterns and works toward a limited treatment goal.", text: "Tiefenpsychologisch fundierte Psychotherapie (psychodynamic psychotherapy) is a procedure that developed in Germany within the framework of the Psychotherapy Guideline. It shares the theoretical foundations of psychoanalysis but limits treatment to working on a central, currently active conflict or on structural limitations connected with the symptoms.\n\nTreatment usually takes place in a seated position at a frequency of one session per week. Regressive processes tend to be limited; transference is taken into account but developed less systematically than in psychoanalytic psychotherapy. Variants include focal, brief and dynamic psychotherapies, as well as low-frequency therapy within a longer-term, supportive relationship.\n\nUnder the Psychotherapy Guideline, long-term therapy for adults usually comprises up to 60 sessions, and up to 100 sessions in special cases. Alongside behavior therapy, psychodynamic psychotherapy is one of the procedures most frequently used in outpatient care. It is a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren, reimbursed by statutory health insurance)." },
  { key:"Analytische Psychotherapie", begriff: "Psychoanalytic Psychotherapy (Analytische Psychotherapie)", kurz: "Psychoanalytically based guideline procedure that changes not only the symptoms but also the underlying neurotic structure through the development and working through of transference, resistance and regression.", text: "Analytische Psychotherapie (psychoanalytic psychotherapy) is the form of psychoanalysis anchored in the German statutory health insurance system. Its aim is a comprehensive change in personality structure by reviving, understanding and working through early, unconscious conflicts within the therapeutic relationship.\n\nTreatment takes place at a higher frequency (for adults usually two to three sessions per week), often lying down, and makes use of free association, analysis of transference and resistance, and therapeutic regression. Under the Psychotherapy Guideline, up to 160 sessions, and up to 300 sessions in special cases, can usually be approved for adults.\n\nSpecialist training programs represent psychoanalytic orientations of various schools, including Freudian, Jungian (analytical psychology) and Adlerian (individual psychology). The efficacy of long-term psychoanalytic treatments is supported predominantly by naturalistic and quasi-experimental studies." },
  { key:"Individualpsychologie", begriff: "Individual Psychology", kurz: "Depth-psychological school founded by Alfred Adler that understands the human being as a goal-directed, social unity and places feelings of inferiority, social interest and lifestyle at its center.", text: "Alfred Adler, originally a member of Sigmund Freud’s circle, broke with psychoanalysis in 1911 and developed individual psychology. The name emphasizes the indivisibility (Latin individuum) of the person: thinking, feeling and acting are understood as expressions of a unified, goal-directed style of life.\n\nCentral concepts are the feeling of inferiority and its counterbalancing through compensation, the striving for superiority or perfection, the finality of mental processes, and social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl) as the criterion of mental health. Therapeutically, the focus is on understanding faulty goals anchored in the lifestyle and encouraging their correction.\n\nIndividual psychology influenced education, child guidance and later schools of therapy. Viktor E. Frankl belonged to Adler’s circle in the 1920s before developing an independent approach in logotherapy. In Germany, it is integrated into the psychoanalytically based procedures." },
  { key:"Analytische Psychologie", begriff: "Analytical Psychology", kurz: "Depth-psychological school founded by Carl Gustav Jung with the concepts of the collective unconscious, archetypes and individuation.", text: "Carl Gustav Jung initially worked closely with Sigmund Freud but broke with him in 1913, partly because of differing views on libido and the significance of the unconscious. He subsequently developed analytical psychology.\n\nJung distinguishes a personal unconscious, whose contents he described as emotionally toned complexes, from a collective unconscious containing universal human patterns (archetypes) such as the shadow, anima/animus and the self. The lifelong process of individuation aims at integrating conscious and unconscious parts of the personality. His typology (introversion/extraversion and four psychological functions) is also well known.\n\nTherapeutically, Jungian analysis works with dreams, imagination (active imagination) and symbols. In Germany, it is applied within the framework of psychoanalytic psychotherapy; its specific concepts have been tested empirically only to a limited extent." },
  { key:"Systemische Therapie", begriff: "Systemic Therapy", kurz: "Psychotherapy procedure that understands and treats psychological symptoms in the context of social systems and their patterns of communication and relationship; a guideline procedure in Germany since 2020.", text: "Systemic therapy developed from the 1950s onward out of family therapy, communication theory (the Palo Alto group around Gregory Bateson and Paul Watzlawick), cybernetics and, later, constructivism. Important schools are structural therapy (Salvador Minuchin), strategic therapy (Jay Haley), the Milan approach (Mara Selvini Palazzoli et al.) and solution-focused therapy (Steve de Shazer, Insoo Kim Berg).\n\nSymptoms are understood not as a property of an individual but as an expression and part of circular interactions in relevant systems (family, partnership, work). Typical methods are circular questioning, genogram work, reframing, sculpture work, resource and solution orientation, and the reflecting team. Treatment can take place in individual, couple, family or group settings.\n\nThe Scientific Advisory Board on Psychotherapy recognized systemic therapy scientifically in 2008; the Federal Joint Committee included it in the Psychotherapy Guideline for adults in 2019 (available in care since 2020) and later extended recognition to children and adolescents. Long-term therapy for adults usually comprises up to 36 sessions, with a maximum of 48." },
  { key:"Familientherapie", begriff: "Family Therapy", kurz: "Psychotherapeutic setting and approach in which several family members are treated together and family interaction patterns are the focus.", text: "Family therapy emerged in the 1950s when researchers and clinicians began to consider mental disorders (initially especially in children, adolescents and people with schizophrenia) in connection with family patterns of communication and relationship.\n\nIn addition to systemic family therapies, there are psychoanalytic (e.g., Horst-Eberhard Richter, Helm Stierlin), behavioral and psychoeducational approaches. Well-studied treatments for children and adolescents include family-based treatment for anorexia nervosa as well as multisystemic and functional family therapies for conduct disorders and substance use.\n\nPsychoeducational family interventions for psychoses and bipolar disorders, which aim to reduce the risk of relapse, are also well supported and recommended in guidelines." },
  { key:"Lösungsorientierte Kurzzeittherapie", begriff: "Solution-Focused Brief Therapy", kurz: "Systemic approach developed by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg that focuses on the construction of solutions and existing resources rather than on problem analysis.", text: "Solution-focused brief therapy was developed in the 1980s at the Brief Family Therapy Center in Milwaukee. Its basic assumption is that solutions do not necessarily require detailed knowledge of how a problem arose and that clients already possess resources and exceptions to the problem.\n\nTypical interventions are the miracle question, scaling questions, the search for exceptions, coping questions, as well as compliments and observation tasks. Treatment is usually designed to last only a few sessions.\n\nThe approach is widely used in psychotherapy, counseling, youth welfare services and coaching. The evidence base is heterogeneous; reviews find predominantly positive, mostly small to moderate effects, with study quality often methodologically limited." },
  { key:"Gesprächspsychotherapie", begriff: "Client-Centered Psychotherapy (Gesprächspsychotherapie)", kurz: "Humanistic approach founded by Carl Rogers (client-centered or person-centered psychotherapy) that enables change through empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence.", text: "Client-centered or person-centered psychotherapy was developed by Carl Rogers in the 1940s and 1950s; in German-speaking countries it became known as Gesprächspsychotherapie, above all through Reinhard and Anne-Marie Tausch. It assumes an actualizing tendency inherent in human beings. Mental disorders are understood as incongruence between self-concept and organismic experience.\n\nAccording to Rogers, three relational conditions are central: empathic understanding, unconditional positive regard and genuineness (congruence) on the part of the therapist. These basic attitudes have shaped conversational practice far beyond psychotherapy and are regarded as important components of the therapeutic relationship in almost all approaches.\n\nIn Germany, client-centered psychotherapy was scientifically recognized for the treatment of adults by the Scientific Advisory Board on Psychotherapy in 2002; the Federal Joint Committee rejected its inclusion as a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren) in 2008. A further development is Emotion-Focused Therapy." },
  { key:"Gestalttherapie", begriff: "Gestalt Therapy", kurz: "Humanistic-experiential approach founded by Fritz and Laura Perls and Paul Goodman that centers on awareness in the here and now and on contact between person and environment.", text: "Gestalt therapy emerged in the 1940s and 1950s under the influence of psychoanalysis, Gestalt psychology, phenomenology, existential philosophy and holism. The book by Perls, Hefferline and Goodman published in 1951 is considered its foundational work.\n\nCentral concepts are awareness, the contact cycle between organism and environment, figure-ground processes and the completion of “open gestalts,” that is, unfinished situations. Typical methods are empty-chair work, dialogues between inner parts, experiments, body awareness and consistently staying with present experience.\n\nIn Germany, Gestalt therapy is not a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren). Some of its techniques, especially chair work, have been adopted into Emotion-Focused Therapy and Schema Therapy and studied empirically there." },
  { key:"Psychodrama", begriff: "Psychodrama", kurz: "Action-oriented approach founded by Jacob L. Moreno in which life situations, inner conflicts and relationships are enacted and worked through scenically.", text: "Jacob Levy Moreno developed psychodrama from the 1920s onward in Vienna and later in the United States; he is also regarded as a co-founder of group psychotherapy and sociometry. It is based on the assumption that spontaneity and creativity are central health factors and that action opens up its own access to experience.\n\nA psychodrama session is divided into a warm-up, an action phase and an integration or sharing phase. The protagonist enacts a scene on the stage; other participants take on roles as auxiliary egos. Core techniques are role reversal, doubling, mirroring and future projection. The approach is used in group settings but also in individual settings (monodrama).\n\nIn Germany, psychodrama is not a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren); individual techniques are used in other approaches. The research base consists predominantly of smaller and methodologically heterogeneous studies." },
  { key:"Transaktionsanalyse", begriff: "Transactional Analysis", kurz: "Model of personality and communication founded by Eric Berne with the ego states of Parent, Adult and Child, applied in psychotherapy, counseling and organizations.", text: "Eric Berne, originally trained in psychoanalysis, developed transactional analysis (TA) in the 1950s. It describes personality in terms of three ego states: the Parent (adopted attitudes), the Adult (reality-oriented processing) and the Child (patterns of experience and behavior from childhood).\n\nCommunication is analyzed as a sequence of transactions between ego states (complementary, crossed and ulterior transactions). Further concepts are psychological games, life positions (“I’m OK – You’re OK”) and the life script, an unconscious life plan formed in childhood. Therapeutically, a treatment contract and the strengthening of the Adult ego state are emphasized.\n\nIn Germany, TA is not a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren) and is used primarily in counseling, education and organizational development. Controlled efficacy studies are available only to a limited extent." },
  { key:"Hypnotherapie", begriff: "Hypnotherapy", kurz: "Psychotherapeutic use of hypnotic states of focused attention and heightened suggestibility, particularly in the tradition of Milton H. Erickson.", text: "Hypnosis refers to a state of focused, inwardly directed attention with reduced awareness of the surroundings and heightened receptivity to suggestions. Modern hypnotherapy was shaped decisively by Milton H. Erickson, who emphasized indirect, permissive suggestions, metaphors and the utilization of patients’ resources and individual characteristics.\n\nHypnotherapeutic interventions are used, among other things, for pain, psychosomatic complaints, anxiety, smoking cessation and in trauma therapy, usually as a method within a more comprehensive treatment plan. Elements are also found in imaginative approaches.\n\nThe Scientific Advisory Board on Psychotherapy has recognized hypnotherapy as a method for circumscribed areas of application in adults; it is not a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren), but hypnosis is provided for as a suggestive intervention in the Psychotherapy Guideline. There is a positive evidence base for medical hypnosis, for example in irritable bowel syndrome and during medical procedures." },
  { key:"EMDR", begriff: "EMDR", kurz: "Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing: a trauma-focused method developed by Francine Shapiro that combines confrontation with distressing memories with bilateral stimulation.", text: "EMDR was developed by Francine Shapiro in the late 1980s. The standardized procedure comprises eight phases: history taking and treatment planning, stabilization and preparation, assessment of the target memory, desensitization and reprocessing, installation, body scan, closure, and reevaluation. While the patient activates the memory, guided eye movements or other bilateral stimuli (tactile, auditory) are used.\n\nShapiro explains its effect with the adaptive information processing model. Among other explanations, a taxing of working memory that reduces the vividness and emotionality of the memory is discussed; whether the eye movements make a specific contribution beyond the exposure component remains controversial.\n\nEMDR is regarded as an effective treatment for posttraumatic stress disorder and is recommended alongside trauma-focused CBT in international guidelines and in the German S3 guideline. In Germany, the Federal Joint Committee approved EMDR in 2014 as a method within the guideline procedures (Richtlinienverfahren) for the treatment of PTSD in adults." },
  { key:"Traumafokussierte Kognitive Verhaltenstherapie", begriff: "Trauma-Focused Cognitive Behavioral Therapy", kurz: "Umbrella term for cognitive-behavioral treatments that directly address the processing of traumatic memories and their meanings; also the name of a program for children and adolescents (TF-CBT).", text: "In the broader sense, trauma-focused cognitive behavioral therapy encompasses approaches such as Prolonged Exposure, Cognitive Processing Therapy and Ehlers and Clark’s Cognitive Therapy for PTSD. These target the memory of the trauma, problematic appraisals of the trauma and its consequences, and maintaining avoidance behavior.\n\nIn the narrower sense, TF-CBT (Trauma-Focused Cognitive Behavioral Therapy) refers to the program developed by Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger for traumatized children and adolescents with the involvement of caregivers. Its components include psychoeducation, relaxation, affect regulation, cognitive coping, the creation of a trauma narrative, in vivo exposure, conjoint parent-child sessions and enhancing future safety.\n\nTrauma-focused CBT approaches are among the best-supported treatments for posttraumatic stress disorder and are recommended in guidelines as first-line treatment." },
  { key:"Kognitive Verarbeitungstherapie", begriff: "Cognitive Processing Therapy", kurz: "Cognitive Processing Therapy (CPT): a manualized cognitive therapy for PTSD developed by Patricia Resick that identifies and modifies trauma-related “stuck points.”", text: "Cognitive Processing Therapy was developed by Patricia A. Resick, initially for survivors of sexual violence. It assumes that posttraumatic stress disorder results from blocked processing of the trauma, in which those affected either assimilate the event to existing beliefs (e.g., self-blame) or change their beliefs in an overgeneralized way.\n\nThe protocol usually comprises twelve sessions. After psychoeducation, patients write an impact statement on the meaning of the trauma; subsequently, unhelpful cognitions (“stuck points”) are worked on using Socratic questions and worksheets, particularly regarding the themes of safety, trust, power/control, esteem and intimacy. Optionally, a written trauma account is produced.\n\nCPT has been shown to be effective in numerous randomized trials, including with military personnel, and is recommended in guidelines for the treatment of PTSD." },
  { key:"Prolongierte Exposition", begriff: "Prolonged Exposure", kurz: "Exposure-based PTSD treatment developed by Edna Foa involving repeated imaginal confrontation with the trauma memory and in vivo exposure to avoided situations.", text: "Prolonged Exposure (PE) is based on Edna Foa and Michael Kozak’s emotional processing theory. According to this theory, fear is maintained by a fear structure in memory that cannot be corrected because of avoidance; exposure activates this structure and enables the integration of corrective information.\n\nTreatment usually comprises eight to fifteen sessions with psychoeducation, breathing retraining, imaginal exposure (repeated, detailed recounting of the trauma memory in the present tense followed by processing) and graded in vivo exercises. Sessions are recorded so that patients can repeat the exposure at home.\n\nPE is among the most thoroughly studied treatments for posttraumatic stress disorder and is recommended in guidelines." },
  { key:"Narrative Expositionstherapie", begriff: "Narrative Exposure Therapy", kurz: "Short-term treatment for trauma-related disorders following multiple and organized violence that embeds traumatic memories in a chronological life narrative.", text: "Narrative Exposure Therapy (NET) was developed by Maggie Schauer, Frank Neuner and Thomas Elbert, initially for survivors of war, torture and displacement, including in crisis regions with limited healthcare resources.\n\nAt the outset, patients lay out a symbolic lifeline on which positive experiences (flowers) and distressing events (stones) are marked. In the following sessions, the life story is narrated chronologically; for traumatic events, detailed exposure takes place with sensory, emotional, cognitive and bodily details until the fragmented trauma memory is placed within an autobiographical context. The process concludes with a written testimony of the life story.\n\nRandomized trials of NET in various cultural contexts are available, with predominantly positive results; a variant for children is KIDNET." },
  { key:"Imagery Rescripting", begriff: "Imagery Rescripting", kurz: "Imaginative technique in which distressing memories or images are evoked in the imagination and changed so that unmet needs are fulfilled.", text: "Imagery rescripting was elaborated by Arnoud Arntz and Mervin Smucker, among others, and is a central element of Schema Therapy. It is increasingly also used as a stand-alone intervention for posttraumatic stress disorder, social anxiety, depression and nightmares.\n\nThe patient brings to mind a distressing scene, often from childhood, up to the moment of greatest threat. The scene is then rewritten: a helping figure, the therapist or the adult self intervenes, protects the child and ensures that the child’s needs are met. The aim is to change not the historical memory but its meaning.\n\nThe effect is explained by a change in the meanings associated with the memory. Randomized trials support its efficacy, among others in PTSD resulting from childhood trauma." },
  { key:"Exposition", begriff: "Exposure", kurz: "Behavioral method in which patients deliberately expose themselves, without avoidance, to anxiety-provoking situations, stimuli, memories or bodily sensations.", text: "Exposure (confrontation) is one of the most effective interventions for anxiety disorders, obsessive-compulsive disorder and trauma-related disorders. Its basic assumption is that avoidance and safety behaviors reduce anxiety in the short term but maintain it in the long term because they prevent corrective experiences.\n\nA distinction is made between in vivo exposure (in reality), imaginal exposure (in sensu, in the imagination), interoceptive exposure (to bodily sensations) and virtual reality exposure. It can be graded or massed (flooding). Classically, its effect was explained by habituation; today, the importance of inhibitory learning is emphasized, in which new, non-threatening associations are learned that compete with the old fear memory. This gives rise to recommendations such as maximizing expectancy violation, eliminating safety behaviors and varying the exercises.\n\nExposure is a core component of disorder-specific CBT programs and firmly established in guidelines for anxiety disorders, obsessive-compulsive disorder and PTSD." },
  { key:"Exposition mit Reaktionsverhinderung", begriff: "Exposure and Response Prevention", kurz: "Treatment of choice for obsessive-compulsive disorder, in which patients expose themselves to obsession-triggering stimuli while refraining from compulsions and neutralizing.", text: "Exposure and response prevention (ERP) goes back to the work of Victor Meyer in the 1960s. It is based on the assumption that compulsions and mental neutralizing reduce the tension triggered by obsessions in the short term and are thereby negatively reinforced.\n\nIn treatment, after joint planning, obsession-triggering situations are sought out (e.g., touching supposedly contaminated objects) while rituals, reassurance seeking and avoidance are omitted. The exercises are often therapist-assisted, sometimes in the home environment, and are subsequently continued independently; cognitive interventions complement the approach.\n\nCBT with exposure and response prevention is recommended in guidelines as first-line treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder and is well supported by evidence." },
  { key:"Systematische Desensibilisierung", begriff: "Systematic Desensitization", kurz: "Procedure developed by Joseph Wolpe in which anxiety-provoking images are presented in graded steps while the person is in a state of relaxation.", text: "Joseph Wolpe developed systematic desensitization in the 1950s based on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: a response incompatible with anxiety, above all relaxation, was meant to gradually weaken the anxiety response (counterconditioning).\n\nThe procedure comprises three steps: learning a relaxation technique (usually progressive muscle relaxation), constructing an anxiety hierarchy, and the graded presentation of the situations in the imagination, beginning with the least distressing, in each case until the anxiety subsides under relaxation.\n\nSystematic desensitization is among the historically most significant methods of behavior therapy. Later research showed that relaxation is not necessary for the effect and that in vivo exposure is generally more effective; today, the procedure is therefore less frequently used in its pure form." },
  { key:"Kognitive Umstrukturierung", begriff: "Cognitive Restructuring", kurz: "Group of methods in cognitive therapy used to identify dysfunctional thoughts and beliefs, examine them and replace them with more realistic, more helpful appraisals.", text: "Cognitive restructuring is a core element of Aaron T. Beck’s cognitive behavioral therapy and Albert Ellis’s Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy. It is based on the assumption that emotions and behavior are substantially influenced by the appraisal of situations.\n\nThe procedure typically involves recognizing automatic thoughts (for example, with thought records), examining them in Socratic dialogue through questions about evidence, alternatives, consequences and usefulness, developing alternative appraisals and testing them through behavioral experiments. At a deeper level, core beliefs and schemas are addressed.\n\nCognitive restructuring is a component of numerous well-supported disorder-specific programs. Its specific effect compared with behavioral components is debated controversially in dismantling studies." },
  { key:"ABC-Modell", begriff: "ABC Model", kurz: "Model formulated by Albert Ellis according to which emotional and behavioral consequences (C) are determined not directly by activating events (A) but by appraisals and beliefs (B).", text: "The ABC model is the core of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy. A stands for the activating event, B for beliefs and appraisals, and C for the emotional and behavioral consequences.\n\nIn therapy, the model is extended by D (disputation, the questioning of irrational beliefs) and E (effect, the new, functional belief and its impact). Disputation is empirical (Is that true?), logical (Does that necessarily follow?) and pragmatic or hedonistic (Does this belief help me?).\n\nBeyond this, the ABC model is used in many cognitive-behavioral approaches as a simple psychoeducational explanatory model." },
  { key:"Rational-Emotive Verhaltenstherapie", begriff: "Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy", kurz: "Cognitive-behavioral approach developed by Albert Ellis from 1955 onward that traces emotional disturbances to irrational, rigid beliefs and actively disputes them.", text: "Albert Ellis founded his approach in the mid-1950s as Rational Therapy, later Rational-Emotive Therapy; since the 1990s it has been called Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT). He is thus regarded as one of the pioneers of the cognitive turn in psychotherapy.\n\nAccording to Ellis, emotional disturbances arise primarily from rigid demands (“must,” “absolutely should”) and from derived appraisals such as catastrophizing, low frustration tolerance and global devaluation of self or others. The goal is the development of flexible preferences and unconditional self-acceptance.\n\nThe approach is directive, active and educational: irrational beliefs are disputed within the framework of the ABC model; in addition, emotive and behavioral exercises such as shame-attacking exercises are used. Meta-analyses support its efficacy, although study quality is heterogeneous." },
  { key:"Kognitive Triade", begriff: "Cognitive Triad", kurz: "Pattern of depressive thinking described by Aaron T. Beck, involving a negative view of oneself, the world and the future.", text: "The cognitive triad is a central element of Aaron T. Beck’s cognitive model of depression. Depressed individuals see themselves as inadequate or worthless, interpret their experiences in the world as defeats or rejections, and expect difficulties and suffering to continue indefinitely.\n\nAccording to Beck, the triad is fed by dysfunctional schemas acquired in the course of life and activated by stressful events; cognitive distortions such as selective abstraction, overgeneralization or personalization maintain it.\n\nIn treatment, the triad serves as a structuring aid: negative assumptions about self, world and future are identified and examined using cognitive restructuring and behavioral experiments. Hopelessness as an expression of the negative view of the future is also regarded as a significant risk factor for suicidality." },
  { key:"Automatische Gedanken", begriff: "Automatic Thoughts", kurz: "Spontaneous and involuntary, usually brief situation-related appraisals that directly influence emotional experience.", text: "The term was coined by Aaron T. Beck. Automatic thoughts occur quickly and often unnoticed, seem plausible to the person and are rarely questioned. They can be verbal or image-based and are connected with deeper underlying assumptions and schemas.\n\nIn mental disorders, automatic thoughts are frequently characterized by cognitive distortions, such as black-and-white thinking, mind reading, catastrophizing, overgeneralization or emotional reasoning.\n\nIn cognitive therapy, automatic thoughts are recorded using thought records, examined for their validity and function, and supplemented with more balanced appraisals. Questionnaires such as the Automatic Thoughts Questionnaire serve to assess them in research and practice." },
  { key:"Verhaltensanalyse", begriff: "Behavioral Analysis", kurz: "Systematic analysis of the conditions under which problem behavior occurs and is maintained; the basis of treatment planning in behavior therapy.", text: "Behavioral analysis (also functional analysis or analysis of conditions) is the diagnostic core of behavior therapy. At the micro level, it examines which triggering conditions, organismic factors, responses and consequences characterize a specific problem behavior; a widely used scheme for this is the SORKC model.\n\nAt the macro level, overarching factors are considered: learning history, plan and motive structures, social conditions, resources, as well as predisposing, precipitating and maintaining conditions. In addition, self-monitoring records, behavioral observation and standardized assessment are used.\n\nBehavioral analysis leads to a case conceptualization and to the derivation of treatment goals and interventions. It is also part of the report to the expert reviewer in the application procedure of German statutory health insurance." },
  { key:"SORKC-Modell", begriff: "SORKC Model", kurz: "Scheme of behavioral analysis formulated by Frederick H. Kanfer and George Saslow, consisting of stimulus, organism, response, contingency and consequence.", text: "The SORKC model extends the classic stimulus-response scheme of learning theory. S denotes the triggering stimuli or situations, O the organism variable (biological factors, but also enduring cognitive schemas), R the response at the emotional, cognitive, physiological and motor levels, K the contingency, that is, the regularity with which consequences follow, and C the consequences.\n\nWith regard to consequences, a distinction is made between positive and negative reinforcement as well as punishment and extinction, each in the short and long term. Typical of many disorders is that short-term negative reinforcement (e.g., anxiety reduction through avoidance) maintains the behavior even though the long-term consequences are negative.\n\nThe model is widely used in behavior therapy training and practice and was further developed in Kanfer’s self-management approach." },
  { key:"Selbstmanagement-Therapie", begriff: "Self-Management Therapy", kurz: "Behavioral framework approach founded by Frederick H. Kanfer that enables patients to analyze and regulate their behavior independently.", text: "The self-management approach goes back to Frederick H. Kanfer and was elaborated in German-speaking countries together with Hans Reinecker and Dieter Schmelzer. It understands therapy as help toward self-help: the goal is for patients to acquire skills with which they can cope with current and future problems on their own.\n\nThe procedure follows a seven-phase model, ranging from establishing the therapeutic role structure, building motivation for change, behavioral analysis and clarification of goals, through carrying out interventions, to evaluation and termination of therapy. Central techniques are self-monitoring, goal setting, self-reinforcement and stimulus control.\n\nThe approach is less a disorder-specific procedure than a general process model that has strongly shaped behavior therapy training in Germany." },
  { key:"Plananalyse", begriff: "Plan Analysis", kurz: "Case conceptualization method developed by Klaus Grawe and Franz Caspar that analyzes behavior in terms of the conscious and unconscious goals (plans) behind it.", text: "Plan analysis is based on an instrumental understanding of human behavior: behaviors are viewed as means of achieving higher-order goals, which in turn are hierarchically related to basic needs. A frequent starting point is the question of what function a particular interactional behavior might serve for the person.\n\nFrom observations in therapy, the case history and the therapist’s own experience, a plan structure is constructed that makes visible conflicts between plans and the problems resulting from them.\n\nOf practical importance is the motive-oriented or complementary therapeutic relationship derived from it, in which the therapist deliberately responds to the patient’s underlying, acceptable motives in order to strengthen the therapeutic relationship." },
  { key:"Verhaltensaktivierung", begriff: "Behavioral Activation", kurz: "Behavioral treatment method for depression that counteracts withdrawal and avoidance through the targeted scheduling of positively reinforcing and values-based activities.", text: "Behavioral activation goes back to Peter M. Lewinsohn’s reinforcement-based models of depression, according to which a lack of response-contingent positive reinforcement contributes to the development and maintenance of depressive symptoms. Dismantling studies in the 1990s showed that the behavioral component of cognitive therapy alone can be comparably effective, which led to the development of stand-alone programs by Christopher Martell, Neil Jacobson and colleagues.\n\nCore elements are activity monitoring, analysis of the relationship between activity and mood, graded activity scheduling, identification of avoidance patterns and of rumination as avoidance behavior, and orientation toward personal values.\n\nBehavioral activation has been established as an effective treatment for depression in randomized trials and meta-analyses, including in simplified formats delivered by trained non-specialists." },
  { key:"Problemlösetraining", begriff: "Problem-Solving Training", kurz: "Structured procedure for teaching systematic strategies with which everyday problems are defined and solutions are developed, evaluated and implemented.", text: "Problem-solving training goes back to the model of Thomas J. D'Zurilla and Marvin R. Goldfried (1971) and was later developed further by Arthur and Christine Nezu as problem-solving therapy. Its basic assumption is that deficits in social problem solving increase the risk of psychological distress.\n\nThe procedure typically comprises the steps of problem orientation, problem definition and goal formulation, generation of possible solutions (brainstorming), evaluation and selection, implementation and verification of the outcome. In addition to rational problem solving, a constructive problem orientation is fostered.\n\nProblem-solving training is used as a stand-alone brief intervention, for example for depression in later life and in primary care, as well as a building block in numerous programs. Its efficacy for depression has been established meta-analytically." },
  { key:"Soziales Kompetenztraining", begriff: "Social Skills Training", kurz: "Behavioral training for building socially competent behavior, for example in asserting legitimate concerns, in relationships and in making contact.", text: "Social competence refers to the ability to achieve one’s own goals in social situations while giving appropriate consideration to relationships. Deficits in this area occur in social anxiety, depression, psychoses and other disorders and contribute to their maintenance.\n\nTraining programs combine psychoeducation, cognitive interventions, observational learning, role-plays with video feedback and graded exercises in everyday life. Widespread in German-speaking countries is the group training of social competencies (Gruppentraining sozialer Kompetenzen, GSK) by Rüdiger Hinsch and Ulrich Pfingsten, which distinguishes the situation types “asserting one’s rights,” “shaping relationships” and “seeking sympathy.”\n\nSocial skills trainings are usually conducted in a group setting and are a component of numerous inpatient and outpatient treatment programs." },
  { key:"Interpersonelle Psychotherapie", begriff: "Interpersonal Psychotherapy", kurz: "Time-limited therapy for depression developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman that addresses symptoms in connection with current interpersonal problem areas.", text: "Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT) was developed in the 1970s for the treatment of depression. It draws on the interpersonal school of psychiatry (Adolf Meyer, Harry Stack Sullivan), on attachment theory and on empirical findings on the relationship between social stress and depression.\n\nTreatment usually comprises twelve to twenty sessions. After an initial phase in which depression is explained as a treatable illness, therapy concentrates on one of four problem areas: complicated grief, interpersonal disputes (role disputes), role transitions, or interpersonal deficits and social isolation.\n\nIPT is well supported for depression and is recommended in international guidelines and in the German National Disease Management Guideline (Nationale VersorgungsLeitlinie); adaptations exist for adolescents, peripartum depression, bipolar disorders and eating disorders." },
  { key:"Positive Psychotherapie", begriff: "Positive Psychotherapy", kurz: "Name for two different approaches: the transcultural Positive Psychotherapy of Nossrat Peseschkian and the Positive Psychotherapy based on positive psychology by Martin Seligman and Tayyab Rashid.", text: "Nossrat Peseschkian’s Positive Psychotherapy (developed in Wiesbaden from the 1970s onward) is a psychodynamically and humanistically oriented, resource-oriented and transcultural approach. “Positive” refers to the Latin positum, that which is given: in addition to disorders, existing capacities are emphasized. It works with a five-stage procedure, the balance model of life domains and the use of stories.\n\nTo be distinguished from this is Positive Psychotherapy (PPT) developed by Martin Seligman, Tayyab Rashid and Acacia Parks, which builds on concepts of positive psychology. In addition to symptom reduction, it aims at building positive emotions, engagement, relationships, meaning and accomplishment, and uses exercises such as working with character strengths and gratitude interventions.\n\nEfficacy studies are available for both approaches, but the evidence base is smaller than for established procedures." },
  { key:"Positiv-psychologische Interventionen", begriff: "Positive Psychology Interventions", kurz: "Targeted exercises and programs for fostering positive emotions, strengths, the experience of meaning and well-being, based on positive psychology.", text: "Positive psychology was launched in the late 1990s by Martin Seligman and Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi as a research program on well-being, strengths and the good life. Positive psychology interventions translate these findings into concrete exercises.\n\nWell-known examples are writing down three good things each day, the gratitude visit or letter, using personal signature strengths in new ways, writing about one’s best possible future self, and exercises on kindness and savoring. They are used in prevention, health promotion, coaching and as a supplement in psychotherapy.\n\nMeta-analyses show predominantly small to moderate effects on well-being and depressive symptoms; the effects are smaller in methodologically more rigorous studies, and efficacy depends, among other things, on person-activity fit and motivation." },
  { key:"Paartherapie", begriff: "Couples Therapy", kurz: "Psychotherapeutic work with both partners aimed at improving relationship quality or at treating mental disorders in the context of the relationship.", text: "Couples therapy is used both for relationship problems and for mental disorders of one partner when the partnership is significant for their development, maintenance or management (for example, in depression, alcohol dependence or anxiety disorders).\n\nEmpirically well-studied approaches are cognitive-behavioral couples therapy with communication and problem-solving training, Integrative Behavioral Couple Therapy by Andrew Christensen and Neil Jacobson, which combines acceptance and change, and Emotionally Focused Couple Therapy by Sue Johnson, which is grounded in attachment theory. There are also systemic and psychodynamic approaches.\n\nIn German statutory health insurance, couples therapy as such is not a covered service; however, significant others can be involved in treatment, and systemic therapy provides for a multi-person setting." },
  { key:"Gruppenpsychotherapie", begriff: "Group Psychotherapy", kurz: "Psychotherapy with several patients at the same time, in which, in addition to the therapist’s interventions, the group processes themselves are used as a therapeutic factor.", text: "Group psychotherapy is used in all major therapeutic orientations: as behavioral, disorder-specific and psychoeducational groups, as psychodynamic or analytic groups, as systemic or humanistic groups, and as skills training (e.g., in DBT).\n\nIrvin D. Yalom described therapeutic factors of the group, including instillation of hope, universality of suffering, imparting information, altruism, corrective recapitulation of the primary family group, interpersonal learning, group cohesiveness and catharsis.\n\nMeta-analyses show efficacy comparable to individual therapy for numerous disorders. In Germany, group psychotherapy was facilitated by the 2017 reform of the Psychotherapy Guideline; group and individual therapy can be combined." },
  { key:"Internetbasierte Interventionen", begriff: "Internet-Based Interventions", kurz: "Psychological interventions delivered via the internet, apps or video technology, ranging from self-guided programs to guided online therapies.", text: "Internet-based interventions encompass a spectrum from pure self-help programs and therapist-guided programs to video-based psychotherapy and blended-care concepts that combine in-person sessions with online modules. Most of the programs studied are based on cognitive-behavioral principles.\n\nFor guided internet-based CBT for depression and anxiety disorders, numerous randomized trials and meta-analyses are available that show substantial effects; guided programs generally have stronger effects and lower dropout rates than unguided ones. Issues under discussion include indication, crisis management, data protection and transferability to routine care.\n\nIn Germany, certain programs can be prescribed as Digital Health Applications (Digitale Gesundheitsanwendungen, DiGA); psychotherapy via video is a covered service of statutory health insurance under defined conditions." },
  { key:"Digitale Gesundheitsanwendung", begriff: "Digital Health Application (DiGA)", kurz: "Low-risk digital medical device in Germany that has been reviewed and listed by the BfArM and can be prescribed by physicians or psychotherapists and reimbursed by statutory health insurance (DiGA).", text: "The Digital Healthcare Act (Digitale-Versorgung-Gesetz, DVG) of 2019 created an entitlement to Digital Health Applications (Digitale Gesundheitsanwendungen) in Germany (§ 33a of Book V of the German Social Code, SGB V). In a so-called fast-track procedure, the Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM) reviews requirements for safety, functionality, data protection, information security and interoperability, as well as proof of positive healthcare effects, and maintains a public directory (§ 139e SGB V).\n\nApplications can be included permanently or, if proof of positive healthcare effects is still pending, provisionally for a trial period. A considerable proportion of the listed applications concern mental disorders, such as depression, anxiety disorders and insomnia.\n\nDiGA can be prescribed by physicians and psychotherapists or, with a documented indication, applied for directly at the health insurance fund. Issues under discussion include, in particular, the quality of the evidence, pricing and integration into courses of treatment." },
  { key:"Psychopharmakotherapie", begriff: "Psychopharmacotherapy", kurz: "Treatment of mental disorders with medications that act on the central nervous system; distinct from and complementary to psychotherapy.", text: "Psychotropic drugs are classified according to their main indication or mode of action, for example into antidepressants, antipsychotics, anxiolytics and hypnotics, mood stabilizers (phase prophylactics), psychostimulants and anti-dementia drugs. Prescribing them is reserved for physicians; psychological psychotherapists in Germany do not have prescribing authority.\n\nPsychopharmacotherapy and psychotherapy differ in mechanism of action, onset of effect, side-effect profile and durability. For many disorders both are effective; depending on severity, course and preference, guidelines recommend psychotherapy, pharmacotherapy or a combination, for example combination treatment for severe depressive episodes.\n\nFor some disorders, such as anxiety disorders and obsessive-compulsive disorder, there is evidence of better relapse prevention through psychotherapy after the end of treatment; in psychoses and bipolar disorders, by contrast, pharmacological treatment is a central component." },
  { key:"Allgemeine Wirkfaktoren", begriff: "Common Factors", kurz: "Principles of change across therapeutic schools that explain the success of psychotherapy, e.g., the factors described by Klaus Grawe: resource activation, problem actuation, problem mastery, motivational clarification and the therapeutic relationship.", text: "The question of factors common to different psychotherapy approaches was raised as early as 1936 by Saul Rosenzweig and later elaborated by Jerome Frank, who emphasized factors such as an emotionally charged relationship, a healing setting, a plausible explanatory model and a ritual.\n\nFrom his comprehensive analysis of psychotherapy research, Klaus Grawe derived five mechanisms of change: resource activation (use of existing strengths and motivation), problem actuation (making problems experienceable in therapy in a realistic way), active help with problem mastery, motivational clarification (clarification of meanings and motives), and the therapeutic relationship. He advocated a general psychotherapy (Allgemeine Psychotherapie) that transcends schools and is oriented toward principles of change.\n\nThe debate about the relative weight of common and specific factors has not been settled to this day. Modern approaches such as process-based therapy attempt to identify effective processes of change empirically instead of focusing on therapeutic schools." },
  { key:"Dodo-Bird-Verdikt", begriff: "Dodo Bird Verdict", kurz: "Term for the hypothesis that different bona fide psychotherapy approaches are, on average, approximately equally effective.", text: "The term alludes to a scene in Lewis Carroll’s *Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland* in which, after a race, the Dodo announces: “Everybody has won, and all must have prizes.” Saul Rosenzweig used the image as early as 1936; it became well known through the review by Lester Luborsky and colleagues (1975).\n\nProponents, in particular Bruce Wampold, rely on meta-analyses of direct comparisons between well-developed approaches intended to be therapeutic, which show only small differences. They conclude that common factors such as alliance, empathy and expectation explain a large part of treatment success.\n\nCritics point out that for certain disorders (such as anxiety disorders, obsessive-compulsive disorder and PTSD) specific approaches prove superior, that averages across disorders can conceal differences, and that methodological problems such as insufficient statistical power can create an illusion of equivalence. The debate is considered unresolved." },
  { key:"Evidenzbasierte Psychotherapie", begriff: "Evidence-Based Psychotherapy", kurz: "Psychotherapeutic practice that bases treatment decisions on the best available research evidence in conjunction with clinical expertise and the characteristics, values and preferences of patients.", text: "The concept goes back to evidence-based medicine, which David Sackett and colleagues defined as the conscientious, explicit and judicious use of the best available evidence in making decisions about the care of individual patients. In 2006, the American Psychological Association transferred it to psychology as Evidence-Based Practice in Psychology.\n\nThe evaluation of evidence usually follows evidence hierarchies headed by systematic reviews and meta-analyses of randomized controlled trials. In addition, naturalistic studies on effectiveness under routine conditions, process-outcome research and routine outcome monitoring are taken into account.\n\nCritical issues include the limited generalizability of highly selected study samples, the equation of evidence-based with manualized, allegiance effects and the neglect of relationship factors." },
  { key:"S3-Leitlinie", begriff: "S3 Guideline", kurz: "Guideline of the highest methodological development level according to the rules of the AWMF, based on a systematic evidence search and structured consensus-building by a representative panel.", text: "The Association of the Scientific Medical Societies in Germany (Arbeitsgemeinschaft der Wissenschaftlichen Medizinischen Fachgesellschaften, AWMF) distinguishes the development levels S1 (recommendations by expert groups), S2k (formal consensus-building), S2e (systematic evidence search) and S3, which combines both elements.\n\nS3 guidelines formulate recommendations with graded strengths of recommendation (usually “shall,” “should,” “may”), which derive from the quality of the evidence and from clinical considerations. They are developed jointly by scientific societies, professional associations and patient representatives and are updated regularly. A special form are the National Disease Management Guidelines (Nationale VersorgungsLeitlinien), for example on unipolar depression.\n\nOf importance for psychotherapy are, among others, S3 guidelines on anxiety disorders, obsessive-compulsive disorder, posttraumatic stress disorder, borderline personality disorder and eating disorders. Guidelines are decision aids, not binding regulations." },
  { key:"Manualisierte Therapie", begriff: "Manualized Therapy", kurz: "Psychotherapy whose course, content and interventions are described and standardized in a treatment manual.", text: "Treatment manuals have been developed above all since the 1980s in order to make psychotherapies reproducible in randomized trials, to check adherence to the treatment and to be able to transfer results to practice. They exist for numerous disorder-specific, cognitive-behavioral, but also psychodynamic and interpersonal approaches.\n\nAdvantages are transparency, teachability and quality assurance. Criticisms include possible rigidity, neglect of individual characteristics, comorbidity and relational aspects, and the danger of reducing therapy to technique.\n\nA mediating position is the principle of “flexibility within fidelity”: core elements are implemented, but sequence, emphasis and delivery are adapted to the individual case conceptualization. Modular and transdiagnostic manuals take up this consideration." },
  { key:"Therapeutische Beziehung", begriff: "Therapeutic Relationship", kurz: "The totality of feelings, attitudes and interactions between therapist and patient; well studied as a therapeutic factor.", text: "The therapeutic relationship is regarded as significant in all psychotherapy approaches but is understood differently in theoretical terms: as the medium of transference and countertransference (psychodynamic), as a sufficient condition for change (client-centered), or as the basis for carrying out specific techniques (cognitive-behavioral).\n\nEmpirically, the therapeutic alliance in Edward Bordin’s sense, consisting of agreement on goals and tasks and a sustainable emotional bond, is the most thoroughly studied. Meta-analyses show a robust, moderate association between alliance and treatment outcome across approaches and disorders. Further evidence-based relationship elements are empathy, positive regard, congruence, collecting client feedback and repairing alliance ruptures.\n\nIt remains open to what extent the relationship contributes causally to success or partly reflects early symptom improvements; longitudinal studies suggest an independent contribution." },
  { key:"Kurzzeittherapie", begriff: "Short-Term Therapy", kurz: "Time-limited psychotherapy with a focused objective; in German statutory health insurance, a service type comprising up to 24 therapy units.", text: "Professionally, short-term therapy refers to treatments designed from the outset to be of limited scope and focused on a circumscribed focus. Short-term forms exist in all approaches, for example as psychodynamic focal therapy, disorder-specific CBT, Interpersonal Psychotherapy or solution-focused brief therapy.\n\nUnder the Psychotherapy Guideline, short-term therapy comprises two segments (KZT 1 and KZT 2) of up to twelve therapy units each. It does not require expert review and can be converted into long-term therapy if needed.\n\nResearch on the dose-response relationship shows that a considerable proportion of patients experience meaningful improvements within the first sessions, whereas longer treatments may be necessary for chronic and complex disorders." },
  { key:"Langzeittherapie", begriff: "Long-Term Therapy", kurz: "Psychotherapy with a larger number of sessions; in German statutory health insurance, a service type requiring expert review with procedure-specific session quotas.", text: "Long-term therapy is indicated when the type and severity of the disorder, chronicity, comorbidity or structural impairments require a longer treatment process.\n\nFor adults, the Psychotherapy Guideline provides procedure-specific maximum limits: in behavior therapy usually up to 60, at most 80 sessions; in psychodynamic psychotherapy (tiefenpsychologisch fundierte Psychotherapie) up to 60, at most 100 sessions; in psychoanalytic psychotherapy up to 160, at most 300 sessions; in systemic therapy up to 36, at most 48 sessions. Separate quotas apply to children and adolescents. Approval by the health insurance fund usually follows an expert review procedure.\n\nStudies, particularly on long-term psychodynamic therapies, suggest advantages for complex and chronic disorders, although separating the influence of treatment duration from other factors is methodologically demanding." },
  { key:"Probatorische Sitzungen", begriff: "Probatory Sessions (Probatorische Sitzungen)", kurz: "Sessions held before the start of guideline psychotherapy for diagnostic clarification, establishing the indication and examining the fit between patient and therapist.", text: "Probatory sessions serve to continue diagnostic assessment, clarify motivation and treatment goals, establish the indication for a particular procedure and setting, and assess whether a sustainable working relationship can develop.\n\nUnder the Psychotherapy Guideline, at least two and at most four probatory sessions are provided before short-term or long-term therapy, and up to six for children and adolescents and for people with intellectual disabilities. In addition, before treatment begins, a medical consultation report (Konsiliarbericht) must be obtained to rule out somatic causes.\n\nProbatory sessions usually follow the psychotherapeutic consultation hour and can be conducted with different therapists." },
  { key:"Psychotherapeutische Sprechstunde", begriff: "Psychotherapeutic Consultation Hour (Psychotherapeutische Sprechstunde)", kurz: "Low-threshold initial contact in outpatient psychotherapy, introduced in 2017, to clarify whether a mental illness is present and what help is indicated.", text: "The psychotherapeutic consultation hour was introduced with the structural reform of the Psychotherapy Guideline on April 1, 2017. It is intended to provide timely access and to clarify whether there is a suspicion of a disorder of clinical significance and which further forms of help are indicated, such as acute treatment, guideline psychotherapy, medical or psychiatric treatment, counseling or self-help.\n\nBefore guideline psychotherapy, the consultation hour is in principle mandatory (exceptions exist, among others, after inpatient treatment). For adults, up to six units of 25 minutes each are possible, for children and adolescents up to ten. The patient receives written information on the outcome.\n\nThe appointment service centers (Terminservicestellen) of the Associations of Statutory Health Insurance Physicians (Kassenärztliche Vereinigungen) arrange appointments for consultation hours and acute treatment." },
  { key:"Akutbehandlung", begriff: "Acute Treatment (Akutbehandlung)", kurz: "Service type of outpatient psychotherapy, in place since 2017, for timely intervention in acute mental health crises.", text: "Psychotherapeutic acute treatment was introduced with the 2017 structural reform of the Psychotherapy Guideline. It serves the short-term improvement of acute psychological crises and exceptional states and is intended to prevent fixation or chronification; comprehensive work on the underlying problems is not its aim.\n\nIt can be started following the psychotherapeutic consultation hour without an application procedure and comprises up to 24 units of 25 minutes each. If guideline psychotherapy is subsequently carried out, the acute treatment sessions are counted toward the session quota.\n\nTypical content includes stabilization, relief, psychoeducation, crisis intervention and planning of further help." },
  { key:"Rückfallprophylaxe", begriff: "Relapse Prevention", kurz: "The totality of measures intended to prevent the recurrence of symptoms or problem behavior after successful treatment.", text: "Many mental disorders, such as depression and addictive disorders, follow a recurrent course. Relapse prevention is therefore an integral part of the final phase of psychotherapy.\n\nTypical elements are the identification of high-risk situations and early warning signs, drawing up an emergency or crisis plan, consolidating learned coping strategies, booster sessions, and distinguishing between a one-time slip (lapse) and a relapse. The relapse prevention model of G. Alan Marlatt and Judith Gordon was groundbreaking for addiction treatment; for depression, Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy, among others, is well supported.\n\nSince 2017, the Psychotherapy Guideline has also provided for relapse prevention (Rezidivprophylaxe): after long-term therapy, part of the approved session quota can be used, spread out over time, after the end of therapy." },
  { key:"Ressourcenaktivierung", begriff: "Resource Activation", kurz: "Targeted use and strengthening of a person’s abilities, strengths, relationships and motivations as a principle of change in psychotherapy.", text: "Klaus Grawe described resource activation as one of the common factors of psychotherapy: in therapy, patients should experience themselves in terms of their positive possibilities, strengths and goals. Resources include personal characteristics and abilities, interests, values, social relationships, and material and societal conditions.\n\nResource activation can take place at the process level, for example through a therapeutic relationship that builds on the person’s motives, or at the content level, for example through resource interviews, strengths analyses, recollections of successes and the scheduling of positive activities. It is central in systemic, solution-focused and positive psychology approaches.\n\nProcess studies suggest that resource-activating interventions, particularly at the beginning of therapy, are associated with better treatment outcomes." },
  { key:"Fallkonzeption", begriff: "Case Conceptualization", kurz: "Individual, theory-guided explanatory model of a patient’s problems from which treatment goals and interventions are derived.", text: "A case conceptualization (case formulation) combines information from the case history, diagnostic assessment and the therapeutic relationship into hypotheses about the development and maintenance of the problems. It serves as a bridge between general models of disorders and the individual case.\n\nIn cognitive behavioral therapy, predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective factors as well as core beliefs and behavioral patterns are typically integrated; psychodynamic case conceptualizations describe, among other things, central relational conflicts and the structural level of functioning, for example with the help of Operationalized Psychodynamic Diagnosis (OPD). Modern approaches emphasize developing the conceptualization collaboratively with the patient.\n\nThe case conceptualization is dynamic and is reviewed and adjusted over the course of treatment. Studies on the reliability of case conceptualizations show that structured approaches increase agreement between therapists." },
  { key:"Prozessbasierte Therapie", begriff: "Process-Based Therapy", kurz: "Approach proposed by Stefan G. Hofmann and Steven C. Hayes that orients psychotherapy not toward diagnoses and therapeutic schools but toward empirically supported, individually relevant processes of change.", text: "Process-Based Therapy (PBT) arose from criticism of a research and care model that tests treatment packages for categorical diagnoses. Instead, the question is to be asked which core biopsychosocial processes need to be changed in which person under which conditions in order to achieve that person’s goals.\n\nThe framework is the Extended Evolutionary Meta-Model (EEMM), which organizes processes in dimensions such as affect, cognition, attention, self, motivation and overt behavior from the perspectives of variation, selection and retention. Methodologically, idiographic methods such as network analyses of intensive longitudinal data are used to map individual process networks.\n\nThe approach is designed to transcend therapeutic schools and stands in the tradition of the search for common principles of change. Its empirical testing as a treatment model is still in its early stages." },
  { key:"Transdiagnostische Therapie", begriff: "Transdiagnostic Therapy", kurz: "Treatment approaches that target mechanisms shared across disorders and can therefore be used for several diagnoses at once.", text: "Transdiagnostic approaches respond to the high comorbidity of mental disorders and to the observation that similar processes, such as avoidance, rumination, deficits in emotion regulation or negative self-evaluation, are common to many disorders.\n\nA well-known example is the Unified Protocol by David H. Barlow and colleagues for emotional disorders (anxiety, depressive and related disorders). It comprises modules on motivation, psychoeducation about emotions, mindful emotion awareness, cognitive flexibility, countering emotion-driven avoidance behaviors, exposure to physical sensations and emotion exposure. Acceptance and Commitment Therapy and Metacognitive Therapy are also conceived transdiagnostically.\n\nStudies on the Unified Protocol show efficacy comparable to disorder-specific protocols. Transdiagnostic approaches are considered promising for healthcare delivery, especially in cases of comorbidity." },
  { key:"Strukturbezogene Psychotherapie", begriff: "Structure-Oriented Psychotherapy (Strukturbezogene Psychotherapie)", kurz: "Psychodynamic treatment concept developed by Gerd Rudolf for patients with limitations in psychic structural functions.", text: "Structure-oriented psychotherapy assumes that some mental disorders stem less from neurotic conflicts than from developmental deficits in psychic structure, for instance in the capacities for perception of self and others, regulation of self and relationships, emotional communication and attachment. These dimensions are assessed with Operationalized Psychodynamic Diagnosis (OPD).\n\nInstead of interpretive work on unconscious conflicts, the focus is on fostering structural capacities: the therapist takes on responsive, auxiliary-ego-like functions, supports the perception and differentiation of affects, and promotes the regulation of self-esteem and relationships.\n\nThe approach is applied in particular to personality disorders, somatoform disorders and trauma-related disorders within psychodynamic psychotherapy (tiefenpsychologisch fundierte Psychotherapie)." },
  { key:"Narrative Therapie", begriff: "Narrative Therapy", kurz: "Approach developed by Michael White and David Epston that understands problems as part of life stories and helps clients develop alternative, empowering narratives.", text: "Narrative therapy emerged in the 1980s in Australia and New Zealand and is influenced by social constructionism and by Michel Foucault’s analyses of power and discourse. It assumes that people interpret their lives through stories and that problem-saturated narratives, often shaped in advance by society, restrict possibilities for action.\n\nIts central method is externalization: the problem is viewed as something separate from the person (“The person is not the problem; the problem is the problem”). Through the search for unique outcomes in which the problem had no influence, alternative stories are developed (re-authoring) and consolidated through witnesses, letters or documents.\n\nNarrative therapy is applied above all in systemic contexts, in family and child therapy, and in community work. The evidence base from controlled studies is still limited." },
  { key:"Kunsttherapie", begriff: "Art Therapy", kurz: "Creative arts therapies that use visual art-making and other artistic media to foster expression, self-reflection and coping with illness.", text: "Art therapy in the narrow sense works with visual means such as painting, drawing, sculpting and collage; in the broader sense, the creative arts therapies also include music, dance and movement, drama and poetry therapy. Theoretically, the approaches draw on psychodynamic, humanistic, anthroposophical or solution-focused concepts.\n\nThe created work serves as a medium of expression for experiences that are difficult to put into words, as an object of shared reflection and as an experience of self-efficacy. A distinction is made between active forms (one’s own creative work) and receptive forms (viewing art).\n\nCreative arts therapies are particularly well established in psychiatric, psychosomatic and oncological hospitals; they are not a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren). The evidence base is heterogeneous, with indications of positive effects, for example in schizophrenia (music therapy) and in the context of coping with illness, though methodological quality is often limited." },
  { key:"Körperpsychotherapie", begriff: "Body Psychotherapy", kurz: "Psychotherapeutic approaches that systematically include bodily experience, breathing, posture and movement in assessment and treatment.", text: "Body psychotherapy has its roots in psychoanalysis, particularly in Wilhelm Reich, who described character attitudes as bodily armor. Further developments include bioenergetic analysis (Alexander Lowen), biodynamics, Hakomi, Pesso therapy and concentrative movement therapy; body-oriented elements are also found in trauma therapies such as Somatic Experiencing.\n\nWhat the approaches share is the assumption of a unity of body and psyche: emotional experiences and relational patterns are reflected in bodily sensations, postures and movement patterns and can be made accessible and changed via the body. Interventions include body awareness, breath and movement work, work with gestures and postures and, in some approaches, touch.\n\nIn Germany, body psychotherapy is not a guideline procedure (Richtlinienverfahren); body-oriented methods are used above all in psychosomatic hospitals. The evidence base is limited compared with established approaches but is growing." },
  { key:"Biofeedback", begriff: "Biofeedback", kurz: "Procedure in which physiological processes are measured and fed back in real time so that people learn to influence them voluntarily.", text: "In biofeedback, bodily signals such as muscle tension (EMG), skin conductance, skin temperature, respiration, heart rate or heart rate variability, or brain activity (EEG, then referred to as neurofeedback) are recorded with sensors and fed back visually or acoustically. Self-regulation skills are meant to be acquired through operant learning and improved perception of bodily states.\n\nAreas of application are in particular headaches and migraine, chronic back pain, incontinence, stress and anxiety symptoms, and hypertension. Neurofeedback is used primarily for ADHD.\n\nThere is a good evidence base for biofeedback in tension-type headache and migraine. The efficacy of neurofeedback for ADHD is controversial, as effects are smaller in blinded studies. Biofeedback is usually embedded in a more comprehensive behavioral treatment." },
  { key:"Entspannungsverfahren", begriff: "Relaxation Techniques", kurz: "Standardized, practice-based procedures for inducing a state of physical and mental relaxation, in particular progressive muscle relaxation and autogenic training.", text: "Relaxation techniques aim at reducing sympathetic arousal and achieving subjective calm. Among the best known are Edmund Jacobson’s progressive muscle relaxation, Johannes Heinrich Schultz’s autogenic training, breathing techniques, imaginative procedures, hypnosis and biofeedback; meditative practices such as mindfulness exercises are sometimes also included, but pursue different goals.\n\nRelaxation techniques are used for stress management, sleep disorders, tension-type headache, anxiety and psychosomatic complaints, as well as a building block in multimodal programs. They require regular practice.\n\nThe Psychotherapy Guideline provides for autogenic training, Jacobson’s relaxation treatment and hypnosis as practice-based and suggestive interventions. For anxiety disorders, relaxation techniques alone are generally less effective than exposure-based CBT." },
  { key:"Progressive Muskelrelaxation", begriff: "Progressive Muscle Relaxation", kurz: "Relaxation technique developed by Edmund Jacobson in which muscle groups are deliberately tensed and then relaxed one after another.", text: "Edmund Jacobson developed progressive muscle relaxation (PMR, also progressive relaxation) in the 1920s and 1930s based on the observation that mental tension is accompanied by increased muscle tension. By deliberately tensing and then releasing individual muscle groups, awareness of states of tension is to be sharpened and deep relaxation achieved.\n\nIn practice, abbreviated variants (e.g., following Bernstein and Borkovec) are mostly used, which begin with 16 muscle groups and are gradually reduced to a few groups and finally to relaxation by recall. Lars-Göran Öst’s applied relaxation deliberately transfers this ability to everyday situations.\n\nPMR is easy to learn and is used for stress, sleep disorders, tension-type headache, chronic pain and anxiety; historically, it was a component of systematic desensitization." },
  { key:"Autogenes Training", begriff: "Autogenic Training", kurz: "Relaxation technique developed by Johannes Heinrich Schultz, based on autosuggestion, in which standardized formulas trigger bodily relaxation responses.", text: "The Berlin psychiatrist Johannes Heinrich Schultz developed autogenic training from observations of hypnotized persons and published it in 1932. Through concentrative self-relaxation, a shift similar to hypnosis is to be achieved that the person can bring about independently.\n\nThe basic level comprises six exercises with formulaic resolutions concerning heaviness, warmth, the heart, breathing, the solar plexus (abdominal warmth) and coolness of the forehead, supplemented by a calming formula and a taking-back procedure. At the advanced level, imaginative elements are incorporated. The technique is usually learned in courses over several weeks and requires daily practice.\n\nAutogenic training is provided for in the Psychotherapy Guideline as a practice-based intervention and is widely used in prevention, psychosomatic medicine and rehabilitation. Evidence of efficacy is available in particular for stress and tension symptoms, sleep disorders and functional complaints." }
]);
